const H=1.1,e="case-07",N=7,t="The Trapdoor Reveal",n="A Bizzing mystery",W=3,o="playhouse",a="the Playhouse Quarter: the open-air theatre, the wings, the prompt corner, under the stage",s=["sequence","pronoun","inference","figurative"],r="LIES",F=7,i={id:"brass-lantern",name:"a small brass lantern",note:"made by Mr Dunmore from offcuts of the trapdoor's old catch",art:"a little brass lantern, no bigger than an egg cup, made from the old trapdoor catch, with a tiny bulb inside"},h=[{thread:"A",beat:'a Blot calling card in the programme pile: "A word can fall through a gap and still be there. — B."',docs:["7.8"]},{thread:"A",kind:"seed",beat:"Seed (Odile): in opening panel 1, one of the few guests in the empty stalls is, unremarked, a tall woman in a green coat with a long grey plait, a violin case at her feet. Paint her as one ordinary guest among the rest: never lit, centred or singled out, and absent from every other panel. Nobody mentions her.",docs:[],cutscenePanel:1},{thread:"A",kind:"seed",beat:"Seed (Odile), Interlude: a violin in the dark beyond the stalls plays half a slow tune and stops, as if waiting for an answer. Ada goes still. Nobody sees who. Ada plays the same tune, alone, in the last scene.",docs:[],in:"aside (Interlude) and epilogue"},{thread:"B",beat:`Crane's "inspector" was secretly measuring the theatre for The Inkwell Experience`,docs:["7.7","7.S4.Q4"]},{thread:"C",beat:"FELIX OVERCOMES HIS STAGE FRIGHT and gives the reveal speech on stage (The Podium link)",docs:[],speech:"accusation.speech"},{thread:"C",kind:"speech-plan",beat:"Felix plans his speech (The Podium link)",docs:[],instructions:`Before the reveal, Felix hands the new detective six cards. "Help me put them in order. Hook, three points, close. One of these doesn't belong." The child arranges them on a little music stand; the order is checked, gently, and any order with the hook first, the close last and the three points in any order between them is accepted.`,cards:[{text:"The lights went out for forty seconds, and a lantern vanished.",slot:"hook"},{text:"What Sunny heard: 'It went down like a stone.'",slot:"point"},{text:"Where the lantern stood: DSC is trap three.",slot:"point"},{text:"Who was really in the wings: 'he had already left'.",slot:"point"},{text:"A lantern lit below lights those above.",slot:"close"},{text:"Footnote: a complete history of trapdoors, 1500 to today.",slot:"bin",felix:"One day."}],accept:"hook first, close last, the three points in any order between them"},{thread:"C",beat:`Mrs Achterberg's proverb: "A lantern lit below lights those above."`,docs:[]},{thread:"C",beat:"Nell refuses to rate a hunch until she has read everything (the lesson of Case 6 lands)",docs:[]}],l=[],d={text:`Dress rehearsal at the Playhouse. The Founder's Lantern glows at the front of the stage.
The lights go out for forty seconds. When they come back, the lantern has gone.
The gates were locked. Nothing left the theatre.
One visitor says he saw a thief.
Curtain up is tomorrow at seven. No lantern, no show.`,readAloud:!1,readAloudNote:"Read aloud at the child's choice."},c=[{panel:1,place:"The Playhouse, Tuesday night, 8:01. Opening night is tomorrow at seven.",picture:"A lit stage under the open sky and strings of bulbs that sway in a wind off the harbour; moths circling the bulbs; a smell of sawdust and warm dust from the lamps. Empty stalls except for a few guests (one of them, unremarked, a tall woman in a green coat with a long grey plait, a violin case at her feet). On stage, Celeste, as Wren, sets a glowing brass lantern down at the front. In the prompt corner, half hidden by the curtain, Felix follows the script with one finger.",lines:[{who:"FELIX",direction:"whispering, to himself",text:"Footnote: the prompt corner is the safest seat in a theatre. Nobody can see you."}],clock:"Curtain up tomorrow at seven."},{panel:2,place:"8:02. Blackout.",picture:"A panel that is nearly all black. Down centre, one small square of green glow. Two small live-text sounds: *clunk*, and, a moment late, a long rumble of thunder.",lines:[],sounds:["clunk","a long rumble of thunder (a moment late)"]},{panel:3,place:"Lights up, cold.",picture:"The front of the stage is empty. Petra, headset askew. A minute later, a neat man in a brown suit steps out of the wings, a little out of breath, smoothing his tie.",lines:[{who:"PETRA",text:"The Founder's Lantern! Where's the *lantern*?"},{who:"PETRA",text:"It's been on this stage every night for a hundred and fifty years. No lantern, no opening!"},{who:"STAGE",text:"A minute later, a neat man in a brown suit steps out of the wings, a little out of breath, smoothing his tie."},{who:"HALE",text:"I saw the whole thing. Quite clearly. It was the lighting operator."}]},{panel:4,place:"The agency, on stage.",picture:"Constable Tully at the gate behind them with his cocoa flask, Nell opens her mouth. Shuts it. Opens it again.",lines:[{who:"TULLY",text:"Gates locked since seven. I've been on them. Nobody's gone out, and nothing has either."},{who:"NELL",text:"Hunch-o-meter: … No. I'm not rating it. Not till I've read everything."},{who:"QUILL",text:"Good. *(A pause.)* Very good."},{who:"ASHA",text:"Timeline from seven fifty-eight. Supper was at quarter past six. Dhal."},{who:"DEV",direction:"whispering",text:"My turn with the torch."},{who:"ASHA",direction:"whispering",text:"There are forty stage lights on."},{who:"DEV",text:"Still my turn."}]},{panel:5,place:"The prompt corner.",picture:"Felix, sneezing in the dust.",lines:[{who:"FELIX",text:"I was right here. I saw nothing. It was dark. Footnote: that's what dark is *for*."},{who:"NELL",text:"When we solve it, you're telling it. Out there. On the stage."},{who:"FELIX",direction:"very pale",text:"Footnote to the footnote: no."}]}],u=[{id:"sunny",name:"Sunny Marchetti",age:19,role:"suspect",roleNote:"red herring · lighting operator",look:"Short hair dyed sky-blue, a roll of tape on her wrist like a bracelet, a flask of tea",manner:'Cheerful, exact, says "lovely" about lights and nothing else',expressions:["calm","amused","nervous","offended","relieved"],unlockedBy:null,motive:"She cracked the lantern's glass last week, and a lantern that vanishes can't show a crack",secret:"She hid in the box and cried about it; Mr Dunmore mended it and never told a soul"},{id:"dunmore",name:"Mr Gus Dunmore",age:63,role:"suspect",roleNote:"props master · Tully's false solution",look:"Sawdust in his eyebrows, a pencil behind each ear, a key ring as big as a saucer",manner:"Grumbles in measurements; nobody reads his notes, and he says so",expressions:["calm","offended","worried","guilty","relieved"],unlockedBy:null,motive:"He wants the lantern off the stage and behind glass, and he carries every key in the building",secret:"He has built it a glass case, a surprise for the Playhouse's hundred-and-fiftieth birthday, hidden under a dust sheet in his workshop"},{id:"celeste",name:"Celeste Fairweather",role:"suspect",roleNote:"the lead (returning from Case 5)",look:"Silver-streaked hair pinned up, a lighthouse keeper's daughter's oilskin, a scarf for her voice",manner:'Dramatic, generous, calls everyone "darling"; signs her notes "—C."',expressions:["calm","amused","offended","relieved"],unlockedBy:null,motive:"The last hands on the lantern, gone the instant before the dark",secret:"She has been afraid of blackouts since she was six, and Petra always times her exit so she is never on stage for one"},{id:"hale",name:"Mr Lionel Hale",role:"suspect",roleNote:'"inspector" from Crane Holdings',look:"Neat brown suit, a clipboard he never writes on in public, a silver tape measure on his belt",manner:'Polite and smooth, a little too sure; says "quite"',expressions:["calm","nervous","offended","guilty","relieved"],unlockedBy:null,motive:"Mr Crane wants every room measured by Friday, and nobody must know",secret:"He was in the Playhouse youth company as a boy (he played the cat in the pantomime), so he knows every door in the theatre in the dark"},{id:"petra",name:"Petra Nwosu",role:"witness",roleNote:"stage manager",look:"Headset round her neck, a pencil in her hair, the prompt book under one arm",manner:"Brisk; writes everything down; agrees with everyone in the margin",expressions:["calm","worried","relieved"],unlockedBy:null},{id:"cat",name:"The stage-door cat",role:"witness",roleNote:"Comic witness",look:"Fat, ginger, one torn ear, Inkwell's most famous cat",manner:"Knocks things over; brings things back",expressions:[],portraitNote:"one pose; a tail-flick animation",unlockedBy:null},{id:"ada",name:"Inspector Ada Holloway (retired)",role:"helper",roleNote:"Mentor · in the stalls as Celeste's guest",look:"Velvet jacket, a flask of tea (cold), Semicolon in a covered cage",manner:"Watches; says almost nothing this case",expressions:["amused","calm","thoughtful"],unlockedBy:null},{id:"achterberg",name:"Mrs Winifred Achterberg",role:"helper",roleNote:"Ada's housekeeper · in the stalls",look:"Round, a hat with a cherry on it, a paper bag of humbugs",manner:"Speaks only in proverbs",expressions:["calm","amused"],unlockedBy:null},{id:"tully",name:"Constable Bram Tully",role:"helper",look:"Cocoa flask, notebook with a dog doodle",manner:"Ten minutes behind, never jealous",expressions:["calm","worried","relieved"],unlockedBy:null},{id:"quill",name:"Quill",role:"agency",look:null,manner:null,expressions:[],unlockedBy:null},{id:"nell",name:"Nell",role:"agency",look:null,manner:null,expressions:[],unlockedBy:null},{id:"asha",name:"Asha",role:"agency",look:null,manner:null,expressions:[],unlockedBy:null},{id:"dev",name:"Dev",role:"agency",look:null,manner:null,expressions:[],unlockedBy:null},{id:"felix",name:"Felix",role:"agency",roleNote:"prompting the show since Case 5",look:null,manner:null,expressions:[],unlockedBy:null}],p=[{id:"7.1",chapter:"scene",type:"log",title:"The cue sheet",author:"Petra Nwosu, stage manager",when:"Tuesday, dress rehearsal",where:"the prompt book, open on Felix's stool in the prompt corner",picture:"a ruled page in a fat, battered prompt book; neat ink columns; a pencil note squeezed into the margin; a ring from a mug.",readAloud:!1,body:`ACT 2, SCENE 3: THE LAMP ROOM. Dress rehearsal, Tuesday.
[[c:cue13|Cue 13, 7:58. LX: lights up, warm. Wren enters SL.]]
[[c:cue14|Cue 14, 8:01. Wren sets the Founder's Lantern on the new mark, DSC.]]
[[c:cue-moved|New mark is glow tape. Moved there this afternoon. — P.]]
[[c:cue-exit|Wren exits SL.]]
[[c:cue15|Cue 15, 8:02. LX: blackout. SFX: thunder sheet, Mr Dunmore, SL wings.]]
[[c:cue16|Cue 16, forty seconds later. LX: lights up, cold. Sunny, from the box.]]
*Margin, in pencil:*
[[c:petra-margin|Sunny says cue 15 "went down like a stone". Agreed! Scene needs work. — P.]]`,subtype:"cue sheet"},{id:"7.2",chapter:"scene",type:"notice",title:"Mr Dunmore's notice",author:"Gus Dunmore, props master",when:"Monday",where:"pinned to the crew noticeboard backstage, half covered by a poster for a pantomime",picture:"a cork board crowded with notes; this one in thick carpenter's pencil on the back of a sawn-off piece of card; a drawing pin in each corner.",readAloud:!1,body:`FOR ALL CREW. READ THIS. YES, YOU.
[[c:trap-loose|The catch on trap three is loose.]]
[[c:trap-drop|Put any weight on it and it drops open, then springs shut again.]]
Do not stand on it. Do not put anything on it.
[[c:trap-chalk|I have chalked a cross on it, right in the middle.]]
The new catch comes on Saturday. I mend it then.
— G. Dunmore, props
P.S. Whoever keeps borrowing my good hammer: I know it's the cat.`},{id:"7.3",chapter:"scene",type:"list",title:"The stage plan",author:"Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:'framed on the wall by the stage door, "for new crew"',picture:"a simple painted bird's-eye shape of the stage with three small squares marked on it (no letters in the painting); the words below are live text.",readAloud:!1,body:`THE STAGE: WHERE THINGS ARE. For new crew.
[[c:plan-key|D is down, nearest the audience. U is up, at the back. C is centre.]]
S is stage. L and R are left and right, as an actor faces the audience.
Trap one: USL. Trap two: USR.
[[c:plan-trap3|Trap three: DSC.]]
[[c:plan-under|Under the stage: old costumes, old scenery, spiders. The door is by the scene dock.]]
Thunder sheet: SL wings. Prompt corner: SL, behind the curtain.
[[c:plan-box|Lighting box: top of a thirty-rung ladder, at the very back of the stalls.]]
Foyer and box office: front of house, through the pass door.`,subtype:"stage plan"},{id:"7.4",chapter:"scene",type:"log",title:"The lighting log",author:"Sunny Marchetti",when:"Tuesday, dress rehearsal",where:"on the desk in the lighting box, under a tea flask",picture:"a spiral notebook beside a small lighting desk with sliders; a torch held to the desk with many turns of blue tape.",readAloud:!1,body:`LIGHTING LOG. Tues. Dress. S.M.
7:58. Cue 13. Lights up, warm. Lovely.
8:01. Cue 14. Wren puts the lantern down. So shiny!
8:02. Cue 15. Blackout.
[[c:sunny-clunk|A clunk, down centre.]]
[[c:sunny-stone|It went down like a stone.]]
[[c:sunny-wink|The glow tape winked at me. Lovely.]]
[[c:sunny-thunder|Thunder late AGAIN. Mr D, please!]]
[[c:sunny-16|Cue 16. Lights up, bang on time.]]
[[c:sunny-gone|Lantern gone??]]
[[c:sunny-chair|Never left my chair. Never do.]]
Note to self: buy more tea.`,subtype:"lighting log"},{id:"7.5",chapter:"scene",type:"other",title:"Mr Hale's statement",author:"Mr Lionel Hale",when:"Tuesday, half past eight",where:"written for Constable Tully, who brings it up from the stage door with the cocoa",picture:"a sheet of crisp headed paper (a crane-bird crest, no letters), handwriting very upright and very even.",readAloud:!1,body:`Statement of Mr L. Hale, Buildings Inspector, Crane Holdings.
I came to the rehearsal as a guest.
[[c:hale-wings|I stood in the wings, stage left, all through the blackout.]]
[[c:hale-saw|In the dark, I saw the lighting operator creep onstage and take the lantern.]]
[[c:hale-torch|I could see her by her little torch.]]
[[c:hale-stayed|I did not leave the wings until the lights came up.]]
I hope this helps. A thief should be caught.
L. Hale`,subtype:"statement"},{id:"7.6",chapter:"scene",type:"other",title:"Celeste's statement",author:"Celeste Fairweather",when:"Tuesday, half past eight",where:"handed over in her dressing room, written in green ink on lilac paper",picture:"a lilac note with a pressed violet stuck to one corner; a jar of honey and a scarf on the dressing table.",readAloud:!1,body:`Darlings, here is everything.
[[c:cel-set|I set the lantern on the new mark, exactly as Petra asked.]]
[[c:cel-wobble|The floor gave a tiny wobble under it.]] I thought it was my knees.
[[c:cel-side|When I went on, Mr Dunmore and the inspector were side by side in the wings.]]
[[c:cel-off|Then I came off, stage left, just before the blackout.]]
[[c:cel-left|When I came off, he had already left.]]
[[c:cel-alone|Mr Dunmore was alone at the thunder sheet, waiting for his cue.]]
I went straight to my dressing room to rest my voice. You know why.
—C.`,subtype:"statement"},{id:"7.7",chapter:"scene",type:"other",title:"A notebook page",author:"not signed",when:"Tuesday",where:"the stage-door cat drops it at Felix's feet in the prompt corner, slightly chewed, and sits on it",picture:"a small page torn from a pocket notebook, a corner damp; tidy, very upright handwriting (the same hand as DOC 7.5, painted only as a matching slant).",readAloud:!1,body:`Playhouse. Tues. Private.
[[c:note-foyer|8:00. Foyer. Eighteen paces by eleven. Gift shop goes here.]]
[[c:note-box|8:02. Box office. Too small. Knock through to the bar?]]
[[c:note-back|8:04. Back to the wings. Look interested.]]
[[c:note-crane|Mr C. wants every room measured by Friday.]]
[[c:note-secret|Do NOT tell the theatre.]]`,subtype:"notebook page"},{id:"7.8",chapter:"scene",type:"programme",title:"The programme, with a calling card",author:"the Playhouse",when:null,where:"the top programme of the pile on the front-of-house table; the card is tucked inside its cover",picture:"a folded programme with a painted lighthouse on the cover (no lettering); a white card with one perfect round ink blot slipping out of it.",readAloud:!1,body:`THE PLAYHOUSE, INKWELL.
Tonight: *The Lighthouse Keeper's Daughter*, by Rosalind Ashe.
Dress rehearsal, Tuesday, 7 p.m.
Our founder, Wilhelmina Thorne, lit the Founder's Lantern on our very first night.
It has stood on our stage in every play since.
Our motto is carved over the stage door:
[[c:motto|"A good play ____ only to tell the truth."]]
Lighting: Sunny Marchetti. Props: Gus Dunmore. Stage manager: Petra Nwosu.
Puzzle page: Mme Elodie Van.

*The card inside the cover:*
[[c:card|A word can fall through a gap and still be there.]] — B.`,talk:[{who:"STAGE",text:"Dev runs to the stage door and looks up. The carving over it has a smooth, empty gap."},{who:"DEV",text:"Four letters. The motto doesn't make sense without them. A good play… only to tell the truth?"},{who:"FELIX",text:"It's missing its verb. Footnote: a sentence without a verb is like a play without a stage."},{who:"DEV",text:"Or a stage without a lantern."},{who:"STAGE",text:"End of Chapter 1. Constable Tully comes back from the gates, notebook open."},{who:"TULLY",text:"Every bag searched. Every bin. Every coat pocket. Gates locked from seven, and I was on them."},{who:"TULLY",text:"So it never left. *(He looks at the bare boards.)* It's still in here. Somewhere."},{who:"STAGE",kind:"hook",text:"The stage-door cat pads to the front of the stage and sits beside the glow tape. It stares down at the boards, tail twitching, and will not look away."}]},{id:"7.S1.Q1",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Sunny Marchetti: "Where were you during the blackout?"',author:"Sunny Marchetti",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`In the box. Top of the ladder, back of the stalls.
[[c:sunny-faders|I had both hands on the faders, waiting for cue 16.]]
Forty seconds isn't long. It's very long in the dark, though.`},{id:"7.S1.Q2",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:`Sunny Marchetti: "What did you mean, 'it went down like a stone'?"`,author:"Sunny Marchetti",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`I meant the clunk. Something heavy, down centre.
[[c:sunny-down|Clunk, then it just went down. Then nothing.]]
Petra thinks I meant the scene was a flop. It was a bit floppy.
But that isn't what I wrote.`},{id:"7.S1.Q3",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Sunny Marchetti: "Mr Hale says he saw you take the lantern by the light of your torch."',author:"Sunny Marchetti",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`My torch? [[c:sunny-notorch|My torch is taped to the desk in the box.]] It doesn't come off. That's the point.
And thirty rungs down, then the whole stalls, then back up? In forty seconds?
I'm a lighting operator, not a squirrel.`},{id:"7.S1.Q4",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:`Sunny Marchetti: "Petra says you cracked the lantern's glass last week."`,author:"Sunny Marchetti",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`I did. I polished it too hard. I cried in the box.
Mr Dunmore mended it and said it wasn't my fault.
[[c:sunny-love|I love that lantern. I'd never hide it.]]`},{id:"7.S2.Q1",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Gus Dunmore: "Tell us about trap three."',author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`Catch is loose. I wrote it on the board on Monday, big letters.
[[c:dun-cross|I chalked a cross on it, right in the middle of the stage front.]]
New catch comes Saturday. Till then, nobody stands on it.`},{id:"7.S2.Q2",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:`Mr Gus Dunmore: "Did you know the lantern's mark had been moved?"`,author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`Moved? Moved WHERE?
*(He looks at the prompt book for a long time.)*
[[c:dun-tape|Down centre? Then somebody's stuck glow tape right over my chalk cross.]]
Nobody reads my notes. Nobody ever reads my notes.`},{id:"7.S2.Q3",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Gus Dunmore: "Why was the thunder late?"',author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`Petra says wait three seconds after the blackout. Sunny says go at once.
We argue every night. Tonight I waited.
[[c:dun-bump|I heard a bump under the floor and thought it was the cat.]]
It's always the cat.`},{id:"7.S2.Q4",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Gus Dunmore: "Do you think the lantern belongs on a stage?"',author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`No! It's a hundred and fifty years old.
[[c:dun-glass|It belongs in a glass case, not in a play.]] I've said so a hundred times.
But it isn't mine to move. That's Petra's say, not mine.
*(His eyes go, for a second, to a tall shape under a dust sheet in the corner.)*`},{id:"7.S2.Q5",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Gus Dunmore: "Who was with you in the wings?"',author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`The inspector, at the start of the scene. Stood very close. Asked a lot about walls.
[[c:dun-gone|Then he wasn't there. I didn't see him go.]]
I was counting my three seconds. I don't look about when I count.`},{id:"7.S2.Q6",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:`Mr Gus Dunmore: "What's under the sheet?"`,author:"Mr Gus Dunmore",when:"Chapter 4, in his workshop",where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:"the workshop: shavings on the floor, one warm bulb, a tall empty case of oak and glass, exactly lantern-sized",readAloud:!1,body:`*(He pulls the dust sheet off. Underneath stands a tall case of oak and glass, exactly lantern-sized. It is empty.)*
[[c:dun-case|It's for the Playhouse's hundred-and-fiftieth birthday, next month. A surprise, from the crew.]]
So, yes. I built a box for that lantern. I'm not daft. I know how it looks.
But at cue 15 I was at my sheet, counting to three, like Petra says.
[[c:dun-struck|Then I shook it, late. Ask Sunny. She writes it down every time I'm late.]]`,talk:[{who:"STAGE",text:"The workshop smells of glue and pencil shavings. Tully stands in the doorway with his notebook. Mr Dunmore has his hand on the dust sheet."},{who:"TULLY",direction:"quietly tearing the DUNMORE page out of his notebook",text:"Pencil. You did say pencil."},{who:"ASHA",text:"Late thunder means he was *there*, shaking it. In the dark. With both hands."},{who:"DEV",text:"So Mr Hale wasn't in the wings, and Mr Dunmore was. *(He looks at the empty glass case.)* Then who's got the lantern?"}]},{id:"7.S3.Q1",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Celeste Fairweather: "Where exactly did you set the lantern down?"',author:"Celeste Fairweather",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`On the new glow-tape mark, darling. Down centre, right at the front.
Petra moved it there this afternoon. It looks much better.
It wobbled a little. I thought it was nerves. Mine, not the lantern's.`},{id:"7.S3.Q2",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Celeste Fairweather: "Who was in the wings when you came off?"',author:"Celeste Fairweather",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`Only Mr Dunmore, holding his thunder sheet like a shield.
[[c:cel-gone|The inspector was gone.]] I noticed, because he'd been staring at the walls all scene.
Measuring them with his eyes, darling. Like a man buying curtains.`},{id:"7.S3.Q3",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Celeste Fairweather: "Why did you go straight to your dressing room?"',author:"Celeste Fairweather",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`My voice, darling. Last month it vanished altogether.
I rest it between scenes now. Honey and silence.
*(She folds her scarf, and unfolds it.)*
And the dark. I have been afraid of blackouts since I was six.
[[c:cel-dark|Petra always puts my exit just before the blackout, so I'm never on stage for one.]]
There. Now you know everything. Don't make me lose my voice shouting it.`},{id:"7.S3.Q4",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Celeste Fairweather: "Last time, you hid your own script. Did you hide the lantern?"',author:"Celeste Fairweather",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`*(offended, then laughing)* I hid my script because I was ashamed.
[[c:cel-honest|Since then I've told everyone everything. It's much less tiring.]]
And that lantern weighs as much as a small dog. I can barely lift it.`},{id:"7.S4.Q1",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Lionel Hale: "Where were you during the blackout?"',author:"Mr Lionel Hale",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`In the wings, stage left, as I wrote.
[[c:hale-sure|I was there for the whole forty seconds. I'm quite sure.]]`},{id:"7.S4.Q2",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Lionel Hale: "What exactly did you see?"',author:"Mr Lionel Hale",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`The lighting girl. Operator. I beg your pardon.
With a little torch, creeping across the front of the stage.
It was very dark. But I have excellent eyes.`},{id:"7.S4.Q3",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Lionel Hale: "Why are you at the Playhouse?"',author:"Mr Lionel Hale",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`I'm a guest. Mr Crane admires the theatre. He wishes it well.
[[c:hale-guest|I am not here to inspect anything tonight.]]
I knew this theatre once. A long time ago. That is all.`},{id:"7.S4.Q4",chapter:"interview",type:"transcript",title:'Mr Lionel Hale: "Is this page from your notebook?"',author:"Mr Lionel Hale",when:null,where:"a typed transcript on the desk",picture:null,readAloud:!1,body:`That? Where did you … the *cat*?
*(A long pause.)*
[[c:hale-mine|It's mine.]] I would rather not say any more just now.
I would like to think first. Quite a lot.`}],w=[{suspect:"sunny",questions:[{id:"sunny-q1",type:"where",q:"Where were you during the blackout?",expression:"nervous",expressionThen:"calm",portrait:"nervous → calm",answerDoc:"7.S1.Q1"},{id:"sunny-q2",type:"what",q:"What did you mean, 'it went down like a stone'?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S1.Q2"},{id:"sunny-q3",type:"who",q:"Mr Hale says he saw you take the lantern by the light of your torch.",unlockedAfterDoc:"7.5",expression:"offended",expressionThen:"amused",portrait:"offended → amused",answerDoc:"7.S1.Q3"},{id:"sunny-q4",type:"why",q:"Petra says you cracked the lantern's glass last week.",expression:"nervous",expressionThen:"relieved",portrait:"nervous → relieved",answerDoc:"7.S1.Q4"}],note:"Hale's accusation makes her the first interview"},{suspect:"dunmore",questions:[{id:"dunmore-q1",type:"what",q:"Tell us about trap three.",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S2.Q1"},{id:"dunmore-q2",type:"what",q:"Did you know the lantern's mark had been moved?",unlockedAfterDoc:"7.1",expression:"offended",expressionThen:"worried",portrait:"offended → worried",answerDoc:"7.S2.Q2"},{id:"dunmore-q3",type:"when",q:"Why was the thunder late?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S2.Q3"},{id:"dunmore-q4",type:"why",q:"Do you think the lantern belongs on a stage?",expression:"calm",expressionThen:"offended",portrait:"calm → offended",answerDoc:"7.S2.Q4"},{id:"dunmore-q5",type:"who",q:"Who was with you in the wings?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S2.Q5"},{id:"dunmore-q6",type:"what",q:"What's under the sheet?",unlockedAt:"start of Chapter 4 (after the board)",chapter:4,expression:"guilty",expressionThen:"relieved",portrait:"guilty → offended → relieved",answerDoc:"7.S2.Q6"}]},{suspect:"celeste",questions:[{id:"celeste-q1",type:"where",q:"Where exactly did you set the lantern down?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S3.Q1"},{id:"celeste-q2",type:"who",q:"Who was in the wings when you came off?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S3.Q2"},{id:"celeste-q3",type:"why",q:"Why did you go straight to your dressing room?",expression:"amused",expressionThen:"relieved",portrait:"amused → nervous → relieved",answerDoc:"7.S3.Q3"},{id:"celeste-q4",type:"what",q:"Last time, you hid your own script. Did you hide the lantern?",expression:"offended",expressionThen:"amused",portrait:"offended → amused",answerDoc:"7.S3.Q4"}]},{suspect:"hale",questions:[{id:"hale-q1",type:"where",q:"Where were you during the blackout?",expression:"calm",answerDoc:"7.S4.Q1"},{id:"hale-q2",type:"what",q:"What exactly did you see?",expression:null,answerDoc:"7.S4.Q2"},{id:"hale-q3",type:"why",q:"Why are you at the Playhouse?",expression:"calm",expressionThen:"nervous",portraitNote:"a flicker of nervous; one hand goes to the tape measure on his belt",portrait:"calm → a flicker of nervous; one hand goes to the tape measure on his belt",answerDoc:"7.S4.Q3"},{id:"hale-q4",type:"what",q:"Is this page from your notebook?",unlockedAfterDoc:"7.7",expression:"nervous",expressionThen:"guilty",portrait:"nervous → guilty",answerDoc:"7.S4.Q4"}]}],g=[{id:"D1",type:"pronoun",skill:"pronoun",label:"pronoun",statement:`In Celeste's note, "he had already left" means the inspector. Two men were in the wings, and the very next sentence says Mr Dunmore was still there, alone.`,spans:["cel-side","cel-left","cel-alone","cue15","cel-gone"],why:'"he" could be either man until you read on. The man who stayed is named; so the man who left is the other one. A pronoun is decided by the sentences round it, not only the one it sits in.',required:["cel-left","cel-alone"]},{id:"D2",type:"contradiction",skill:"sequence",label:"contradiction (sequence)",statement:'Mr Hale says he stood in the wings all through the blackout. But "had already left" means he left before Celeste came off, and she came off before the blackout. His own notebook puts him in the box office at 8:02, the minute the lights went out.',spans:["hale-wings","cel-left","cel-off","note-box","hale-stayed","hale-sure","cue-exit","cue15","note-back","note-foyer"],why:"*had already left* is the past perfect: it puts his leaving *before* another past moment. He could not have watched a blackout from a place he had already left. If he wasn't there, he saw nothing, and his story about Sunny is made up.",required:["hale-wings","cel-left","cel-off"]},{id:"D3",type:"figurative",skill:"figurative",label:"figurative",statement:`"It went down like a stone" is not a review of the scene. In Sunny's log, "it" is the clunk down centre. Something heavy really went down.`,spans:["sunny-stone","sunny-clunk","sunny-down","petra-margin","dun-bump"],why:'in a theatre, a joke that flops "goes down like a stone", and Petra read it that way. Read in its own place, straight after a clunk, the words are literal. A figure of speech can also be a plain report.',required:["sunny-clunk","sunny-stone"]},{id:"D4",type:"inference",skill:"inference",label:"meaning (inference: DSC is trap three)",statement:"The lantern's new mark was DSC, down stage centre. On the plan, DSC is trap three, and trap three drops open under any weight and springs shut. The glow tape covered Mr Dunmore's chalk cross. The lantern fell through the trap, and the trap closed over it. It is under the stage.",spans:["cue14","plan-trap3","trap-drop","dun-tape","plan-key","trap-loose","cue-moved","cel-wobble","dun-bump","plan-under","sunny-wink"],why:"each note was right on its own. Petra moved the mark without reading the props board; Mr Dunmore chalked his cross without reading the prompt book. Read side by side, the two notes put the lantern exactly on the one spot that swallows things. Nobody stole it. It is still in the building.",required:["cue14","plan-trap3","trap-drop"]}],y=[{suspect:"sunny",clearedBy:["plan-box","cue16","sunny-notorch","sunny-16","sunny-chair","sunny-love"],note:`Sunny Marchetti is named as the thief in Hale's statement, and she cracked the lantern's glass last week. She ran the lights-up from the top of a ladder at the back of the stalls forty seconds after the blackout began, and the "little torch" Hale says he saw cannot leave her desk.`,suspicion:["hale-saw","hale-torch"],talk:[{who:"ASHA",text:"Thirty rungs down, the whole stalls, onto the stage and back up the ladder in forty seconds? I row for the county. I couldn't."}]},{suspect:"dunmore",kind:"false solution (Constable Tully's, end of Chapter 3)",suspicion:["dun-glass","dun-bump"],clearedBy:["dun-case","dun-struck","sunny-thunder","cel-alone"],note:"Mr Gus Dunmore is Constable Tully's thief at the end of Chapter 3: he wants the lantern behind glass, and he heard a bump under the floor that is his to keep. Cleared in Chapter 4: the case under the sheet is an empty birthday present, and the late thunder that made him look guilty is the proof he was at his sheet in the dark, shaking it."}],m={events:[{id:"T1",order:1,text:"Mr Dunmore chalks a cross on trap three and pins up his notice",spans:["trap-chalk","dun-cross"],placedByChild:!1},{id:"T2",order:2,text:"Petra moves the lantern's mark to DSC with glow tape",spans:["cue-moved"],placedByChild:!0},{id:"T3",order:3,text:"Scene 3 begins; Celeste goes on; Hale and Dunmore side by side in the wings",spans:["cue13","cel-side"],placedByChild:!0},{id:"T4",order:4,text:"Hale paces out the foyer for a gift shop",spans:["note-foyer"],placedByChild:!0},{id:"T5",order:5,text:"Celeste sets the lantern on DSC; it wobbles; she exits; Hale has *already* left",spans:["cue14","cel-left","cel-set","cel-wobble","cel-off","dun-gone"],placedByChild:!0},{id:"T6",order:6,text:"Blackout; clunk; the glow tape winks as the trap drops and springs shut; Mr Dunmore shakes the thunder, late; Hale is in the box office",spans:["cue15","note-box","sunny-clunk","sunny-thunder","sunny-wink","dun-struck"],placedByChild:!0},{id:"T7",order:7,text:"Lights up: the lantern is gone",spans:["cue16","sunny-16","sunny-gone"],placedByChild:!1},{id:"T8",order:8,text:'Hale strolls back to the wings to "look interested"',spans:["note-back"],placedByChild:!0}]},f={culprit:"hale",place:{prompt:"Where is the lantern?",answer:"Under the stage: it fell through trap three",options:["Under the stage: it fell through trap three","In the lighting box","In the foyer and box office","In Celeste's dressing room","In the prompt corner"]},minimalEvidence:["D2","D4","D3"],acceptableEvidence:["D1"],wrongSuspect:{sunny:"Sunny lit the stage from the top of a thirty-rung ladder forty seconds after it went dark. And her torch is taped down. Who said he saw it move?",dunmore:"At cue 15 Mr Dunmore was alone at the thunder sheet. Celeste saw him there, and Sunny heard him shake it, late. A man shaking a sheet of tin does not carry a lantern. And his glass case is empty.",celeste:"Celeste set the lantern down and came off before the blackout. She told us the floor wobbled. That is a clue, not a confession."},weakEvidence:"You have shown a man told a story. A missing thing is still missing. Where does a lantern go when nobody carries it?",question:"Who told a story that wasn't true?"},b=[{who:"PETRA",text:"Oh."},{who:"SUNNY",direction:"both hands over her mouth",text:"It's not even scratched."},{who:"DUNMORE",direction:"from below",text:"Landed in the costume basket. On forty years of petticoats. And there's … hang on."},{who:"DEV",direction:"whispering",text:"My turn with the torch."},{who:"ASHA",direction:"whispering",text:"It's *glowing*. It's a *lantern*."},{who:"DUNMORE",text:"There's a dog down here. Asleep. In the lighthouse keeper's hat."},{who:"TULLY",direction:"rising from the stalls",text:"BISCUIT!"},{who:"QUILL",direction:"to the dog, as he is lifted up through the trap",text:"Lie down, Biscuit. *Lie*, not *lay*. Good boy."},{who:"DEV",direction:"whispering",text:"So *that's* who sighed."},{who:"STAGE",text:"Everyone looks at Mr Hale. He stands up slowly, takes the tape measure from his belt and holds it in both hands."},{who:"HALE",text:"It's true. I wasn't in the wings. Mr Crane asked me to measure every room by Friday. Quietly. For The Inkwell Experience. *(He swallows.)* I knew my way in the dark. I was in the youth company here, at nine. I played the cat. When the lantern vanished, everyone asked who was where. I hadn't been where I should be. I panicked, and I said the first name I could think of."},{who:"SUNNY",text:"Mine."},{who:"HALE",text:"Yours. I'm sorry, Miss Marchetti. It was a cowardly thing to say about someone who works so hard to be seen by nobody."},{who:"SUNNY",direction:"after a moment",text:"You could have just said you were in the foyer."},{who:"HALE",text:"I know that now. It would have been much less tiring."},{who:"CELESTE",direction:"warmly",text:"Darling, that's my line. *(To everyone.)* And I left before the blackout because I'm afraid of the dark. There. Now nobody has a secret."},{who:"FELIX",text:"Footnote: lots of brave people are. Footnote to the footnote: me."},{who:"DUNMORE",direction:"handing the lantern to Sunny",text:"Go on. Find the crack you made. *(She turns it in the light. She can't.)*"},{who:"TULLY",direction:"to Mr Dunmore",text:"I had you down for it. In pencil. I'm sorry."},{who:"DUNMORE",text:"I'd have had me down for it too. The case is for the lantern after all. Just not your way."},{who:"PETRA",direction:"to Mr Dunmore",text:"I moved your mark without reading your board."},{who:"DUNMORE",text:"I chalked a cross without reading your book. *(He holds out a hand. Petra shakes it.)* New rule. We read each other's notes."},{who:"STAGE",text:"Sunny tears her tram ticket in half, very neatly, and hangs her canvas bag back on the bottom rung of the ladder."},{who:"NELL",direction:"to the new detective, quietly",text:"I had a hunch it was the floor. I didn't rate it."},{who:"NEW DETECTIVE",text:"What would you have rated it?"},{who:"NELL",text:"Two. *(She grins.)* I should rate everything two."},{who:"STAGE",text:"In the stalls, Ada is clapping, slowly, with her cold flask under one arm."},{who:"SEMICOLON",text:"Elementary; therefore!"},{who:"STAGE",text:"Felix is still standing down stage centre, off the glow tape. He looks out at the audience, all of them, and for the first time does not look away."}],k=[{who:"STAGE",heading:"The applause.",text:'It goes on long enough that Felix has to bow twice, and the second bow is better. Celeste says, "Darling, you must audition." Felix says, "Footnote: no." Then, after a moment: "Footnote to the footnote: maybe." Quill writes his name on the sign-up sheet for The Podium, in very small, very neat letters, and shows it to him. Felix does not cross it out.'},{who:"STAGE",heading:"The lantern.",text:"On Saturday, Mr Dunmore fits the new catch to trap three and stamps on it nine times. Petra moves the Founder's Lantern's mark back to stage left. The lantern goes on with the show on opening night, at seven exactly, and afterwards, by everyone's vote, into Mr Dunmore's oak-and-glass case in the foyer, where the gift shop would have been."},{who:"STAGE",heading:"Mr Hale.",text:'He gives Constable Tully the rest of his notebook. Tully gives it to Professor Penhallow, who reads it twice and labels it, out loud, "useful". Hale buys Sunny a new tea flask. Sunny lets him carry it up all thirty rungs.'},{who:"STAGE",heading:"The vanished word.",text:"Dev stands under the stage door and reads the carving."},{who:"DEV",text:'"A good play *lies* only to tell the truth." Four letters.'},{who:"FELIX",text:"Footnote: there are two words *lie* in English, from two different old words. One means to tell something untrue. One means to rest flat. Footnote to the footnote: tonight we had both. A man lied, and a lantern lay under the stage."},{who:"ASHA",text:"And a dog lay in a hat."},{who:"STAGE",text:"The new detective writes **LIES** in the Blot Ledger. The ink spreads.",ledger:"LIES"},{who:"DEV",direction:"reading the Ledger aloud",text:"Under the clock that never strikes lies … *(He stops.)* That's almost a …"},{who:"ASHA",text:"Almost a what?"},{who:"DEV",text:"Nothing. Not yet. *(He reads it again, under his breath, all the way home.)*"},{who:"STAGE",heading:"The office object.",text:"A week later, a small parcel comes up in the dumbwaiter, between two cinnamon buns. Inside is a little brass lantern, no bigger than an egg cup, made by Mr Dunmore from the old trapdoor catch. The card says, in thick carpenter's pencil: *For the prompter. Lit below. — G.D.* Felix puts it on the shelf himself, between Dot's green pen and the gavel's old handle, and switches on the tiny bulb inside."},{who:"STAGE",heading:"The last scene.",text:"At No. 9 Lantern Row, rain is ticking on the windows. Mrs Achterberg brings Ada a fresh cup of tea. Ada lets it go cold. She is playing the cello, very softly: a slow tune, and not one from the play. It is the tune the violin played in the dark beyond the stalls. Halfway through, she stops, in the very same place, and waits."},{who:"MRS ACHTERBERG",text:"A borrowed song is sung twice."},{who:"ADA",direction:"not looking up",text:"Felix was very good, Winifred."},{who:"STAGE",kind:"chill",text:"She plays the first bars again, and stops again, in the same place. She listens. Outside there is only the rain."},{who:"SEMICOLON",direction:"very quietly, for once",text:"Elementary; therefore."}],v={kind:"clue-spotter",skill:"figurative",title:"Literal or figurative?",instructions:"Six sentences on chalkboard tiles, each with its own sentence before it. The child decides: does *went down like a stone* mean something really fell, or that something went badly?",items:[{prompt:'"Felix told his best joke. It went down like a stone."',answer:"figurative",options:["literal","figurative"],note:"the joke flopped; jokes do not fall"},{prompt:'"A clunk, down centre. It went down like a stone."',answer:"literal",options:["literal","figurative"],note:"model answer; Sunny's line, shown first",model:!0},{prompt:'"The anchor splashed over the side. It went down like a stone."',answer:"literal",options:["literal","figurative"]},{prompt:'"The new rule about homework went down like a stone."',answer:"figurative",options:["literal","figurative"]},{prompt:'"Dev dropped his biscuit tin off the pier. It went down like a stone."',answer:"literal",options:["literal","figurative"],note:'Dev: "My periscope!"'},{prompt:`"Nell's hunch-o-meter rating went down like a stone."`,answer:"figurative",options:["literal","figurative"],note:'Nell: "Rude."'},{kind:"pronoun",prompt:'"When I came off, he had already left. Mr Dunmore was alone."',answer:"the inspector",options:["the inspector","Mr Dunmore"],quill:"Read on. The answer is in the next sentence.",note:"Then one pronoun tile from Celeste's note. The child taps who he is."}]},x={backgrounds:["The Playhouse stage: an open-air wooden stage under strings of bulbs, a painted lighthouse flat at the back, a rope rail, the wings in deep curtain shadow. Night: the main case setting, lamplit and warm, never spooky. Day: the epilogue, sunlight on the boards. Paint trap three as a square seam in the boards at the front centre, hard to see unless you look; a small strip of glow tape over it in the night version.","The prompt corner: a stool, a little hooded lamp, the prompt book, a curtain edge, dust motes visible in the lamp beam (Felix sneezes here).","The lighting box: a tiny wooden booth on stilts at the back of the stalls, a long ladder up to it, a lighting desk with sliders, a torch taped down with blue tape, a tea flask.","Under the stage: low beams, a wicker costume basket overflowing with petticoats, old painted flats leaning in rows, a square of light falling from the open trap. Cosy, not creepy; one friendly spider in a corner.","The foyer and box office: a small panelled foyer, a ticket window with a brass grille, a bench. In the epilogue, the lantern in Mr Dunmore's oak-and-glass case.","Mr Dunmore's workshop (new): a lean-to across the yard from the stage, shavings on the floor, clamps on the walls, one warm bulb. Version A (Chapter 3 hook, seen through the window): a tall shape under a dust sheet. Version B (Chapter 4): the sheet off, a tall empty case of oak and glass, exactly lantern-sized.","The lane beyond the stalls (Interlude): a cobbled lane, one gas lamp, nobody in it. Wind in the canvas roof over the stalls. The violin is sound only: never paint a player, a shadow or a figure.","The stage door: an arched wooden door with a carved stone band above it. The carving is drawn as decorative bands and a smooth empty gap; no letters are painted. The motto is live text."],props:["Petra's prompt book with a pencil margin note","Dunmore's notice on sawn-off card","the framed stage plan (painted as shapes only)","Sunny's spiral log","Hale's crested statement","Celeste's lilac note with a pressed violet","the chewed notebook page","the lighthouse programme","the Founder's Lantern (brass, glass panes, a ring handle; it glows warm gold in the stage lights)","Hale's silver tape measure","the little brass lantern (the office object)","Mr Dunmore's oak-and-glass case (empty), and its dust sheet"],portraits:["NEW: Sunny Marchetti","NEW: Mr Gus Dunmore",'NEW: Mr Lionel Hale (his "guilty" is a long look at his own tape measure)',"NEW: Petra Nwosu","RETURNING: Celeste (from Case 5)","RETURNING: Ada","RETURNING: Mrs Achterberg","RETURNING: Tully","RETURNING: Semicolon (covered cage, then uncovered)","RETURNING: the stage-door cat"],notes:`Opening panels: panel 2 is black except for one small square of green glow down centre (the glow tape; it is the same in every blackout and is never pointed at). Panel 3: Hale comes out of the wings a beat after the lights, a little out of breath; paint his tie slightly askew. Lettering: none in any painting. Cue numbers, the plan's letters, the motto, the programme and every note are live text. Audience: the few guests in the stalls are ordinary and various, of all ages; camera high enough that faces stay small. In opening panel 1, one guest is a tall woman in a green coat with a long grey plait and a violin case at her feet. Paint her as one ordinary guest among the rest: never lit, centred or singled out, and absent from every other panel. Writer's note on the two "lies". The vanished word is the case in miniature. The Playhouse motto uses lies to mean tells something untrue; the season's sentence will use it to mean rests. In this case a man lies about where he was, and a lantern lies under the stage. Felix names both in the epilogue. Do not explain the double meaning anywhere before the case is solved. Writer's note on Sunny's log (DOC 7.4). "It went down like a stone" must be read by the cast as a theatre review (Petra's margin note in DOC 7.1 does exactly that) before the child reads it literally. Never italicise it, never set it apart. It sits in a list of short lines, like everything else Sunny writes. Felix's speech panels: five panels, one per structure card. Panel 1, Felix tiny on a big stage, lights glaring. Panels 2–4, the camera moves steadily closer and the stage lights soften. Panel 5, Felix full height in warm light, the lantern rising through the trap behind him. In every panel, one face in the front row is the child's avatar.`},T={script:"case-07.md",signedOffBy:"the owner (released at the owner's word, 8 Oct 2026)",signedOffOn:"2026-10-08"},I=[{spans:["hale-saw","sunny-gone"],quill:"One man says he saw her. Was he there to see? Read Celeste's note, and read on past the pronoun."},{spans:["dun-glass","sunny-gone"],quill:"He wants it in a glass case. Wanting is not doing. Where was he at cue 15? The cue sheet says."},{spans:["petra-margin"],quill:"That is Petra *quoting* Sunny. Read what Sunny actually wrote, where she wrote it."},{spans:["dun-glass","dun-bump"],quill:"A wish for a glass case and a bump under the floor. That is Constable Tully's case, in pencil. Where was Mr Dunmore at cue 15, and who heard him there?"}],S=[{span:"card",note:'Blot calling card (arc A): "A word can fall through a gap and still be there. — B."'},{span:"motto",note:"the vanished-word gap: the Playhouse motto, missing LIES"}],A="Mr Dunmore takes the key ring from his belt and goes down through the scene-dock door. A pause. A scrape below. Then trap three drops open with a squeak, and up through it, in Mr Dunmore's two hands, rises the Founder's Lantern, catching the stage lights and glowing gold.",D=[{where:"meta: vanished word",text:'from the Playhouse motto, "A good play ____ only to tell the truth."'},{where:"meta: mystery pass (6 Oct 2026) — clock",kind:"mystery",text:"Curtain up for opening night is tomorrow at seven. No lantern, no show: it has stood on the stage in every play for a hundred and fifty years. Halfway, it gets worse: Sunny packs her bag for the 11:30 tram to her gran's, because everyone keeps looking at the box."},{where:"meta: mystery pass — impossible",kind:"mystery",text:"A lantern as heavy as a small dog leaves the front of a lit stage in forty seconds of dark. The gates were locked from seven, Tully searched every bag, and nothing left the theatre. How? It went down, not out."},{where:"meta: mystery pass — false solution",kind:"mystery",text:`End of Chapter 3, Constable Tully's case against Mr Dunmore (he wants the lantern off the stage, he carries every key, a bump "under the floor" is his, and something lantern-shaped stands under a sheet in his workshop). Broken at the start of Chapter 4 when the sheet comes off.`},{where:"meta: mystery pass — hidden clue",kind:"mystery",text:`Sunny's log, Chapter 1: "The glow tape winked at me. Lovely." Read as Sunny being Sunny; it is the trap dropping open and springing shut under the mark.`},{where:"meta: mystery pass — continuity",kind:"mystery",text:'Hale steps out of the wings a minute after lights-up, out of breath (his notebook: "8:04. Back to the wings").'},{where:"writer's note: the glow tape (DOC 7.4)",text:`Sunny says "lovely" about lights and nothing else, so "The glow tape winked at me. Lovely." must read as character, not clue. Glow tape glows in a blackout; it only winks if the floor under it tips away and comes back. Felix's speech pays it off. Never explain it before then.`},{where:"writer's note: the secrets",text:"Every suspect hides one thing that is not the crime, and each one makes them look worse before it clears them: Sunny cracked the lantern's glass and hid in the box crying; Mr Dunmore has built the lantern a glass case as a birthday surprise; Celeste leaves before every blackout because she is afraid of the dark; Mr Hale grew up in this theatre and knows every door in it blind. Each is told kindly at the reveal."},{where:"Chapter 2 — Interviews",text:"Every answer arrives as a typed transcript on the desk. Questions marked ◆ appear only after the child has read the document named."},{where:"after DOC 7.8",text:"The word is not logged until the case is solved."},{where:"end of Chapter 2",kind:"hook",text:"As the agency crosses the empty stage, the boards give a long, soft creak, though nobody is standing where it came from. Then, from somewhere under their feet, a sound like a sigh.",who:"STAGE"},{where:"end of Chapter 2",kind:"hook",text:"Did the floor just *sigh*?",who:"DEV",direction:"whispering"},{where:"end of Chapter 2",kind:"hook",text:"Floors don't sigh. *(A pause.)* Walk faster.",who:"ASHA",direction:"whispering"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Unlocks after all four interviews.",kind:"unlock"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Ten o'clock. The work lights are on, white and flat. A wind off the harbour gets under the canvas roof over the stalls and makes it breathe in and out, and the strings of bulbs sway and tick against their poles. Far out on the water, a foghorn says one long, low word. Ada and Mrs Achterberg sit in the third row; Semicolon's cage is covered with a tea towel. The agency sits on the edge of the stage, legs dangling, well away from the glow tape.",kind:"stage"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"I *want* to rate it. I've got a hunch, and it's a big one.",who:"NELL"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Is it a ten?",who:"ASHA"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"It's a … I'm not saying. I'm waiting for the evidence. *(She sits on her hands.)* It's very uncomfortable.",who:"NELL"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"It is supposed to be.",who:"QUILL"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"We know Mr Hale wasn't where he said. Probably. But that doesn't tell us where the lantern is. People don't just fall through floors.",who:"DEV"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Footnote: a *trapdoor* is a door in a floor. Footnote to the footnote: the word *trap* here is old. It meant a door that drops, not a … *(He stops. He looks at the floor. He looks at Nell.)* Did someone say I'm doing the speech?",who:"FELIX"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"You are.",who:"NELL"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"I can't. There'll be people. People *looking*. When people look at me my words go and hide behind my teeth.",who:"FELIX"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Mrs Achterberg unwraps a humbug, very slowly, and puts it in her mouth. Then she takes it out again.",who:"STAGE"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"A lantern lit below lights those above.",who:"MRS ACHTERBERG"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Silence. She puts the humbug back.",who:"STAGE"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Is that about the lantern or about Felix?",who:"DEV",direction:"whispering"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Yes.",who:"ASHA",direction:"whispering"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Winifred has never once been wrong. She is rarely quick.",who:"ADA",direction:"to nobody, unscrewing her flask and finding the tea cold"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Elementary; therefore!",who:"SEMICOLON",direction:"from under the tea towel"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"STAGE",kind:"escalation",text:"Sunny comes down the ladder from the box, one rung at a time, with her tea flask under her arm and a canvas bag over her shoulder."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"SUNNY",text:"I'm going to my gran's. Last tram's at half past eleven."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"PETRA",text:"Nobody's sending you anywhere, Sunny."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"SUNNY",text:"Nobody has to. Everyone keeps looking up at the box. *(She sits on the bottom rung, and does not go yet.)*"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"TULLY",direction:"checking his watch, very gently",text:"Twenty-one hours to curtain up. An hour and a half to that tram."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"ASHA",text:"Ninety minutes. *(She writes it on her hand.)*"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"When you speak, do not speak to the room. Speak to one person. Pick the new detective. They have read everything you have.",who:"QUILL",direction:"to Felix, quietly"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:"Will you sit at the front?",who:"FELIX",direction:"to the new detective, very small"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",text:`The child's choice: "Yes" is the only button. It is drawn as a ticket stub: Row A, Seat 1.`,kind:"choice",buttons:["Yes"]},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"STAGE",kind:"blot",text:"Then, from somewhere out in the dark beyond the stalls (not the stage, not the street), a violin begins to play. Slow. A few bars of a tune that is not in the play. It stops in the middle, the way a question stops, as if it is waiting for someone to answer."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"STAGE",kind:"blot",text:"In the third row, Ada's flask stops halfway to her mouth. She does not drink. Under the tea towel, Semicolon says nothing at all."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"DEV",direction:"whispering",text:"Who's playing?"},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"ASHA",direction:"whispering",text:"It's coming from the lane."},{where:"Interlude — the empty stalls (Mrs Achterberg's proverb)",who:"STAGE",kind:"hook",text:"Felix goes to the gate and looks. The lane is empty under its one lamp. There is only the wind, and a faint, sweet smell, like the resin on a fiddle bow. When he comes back, nobody asks, and he does not say."},{where:"notebook gloss (D4)",text:`DSC (stage shorthand): down stage centre, the middle of the stage, nearest the audience. Dev's note: "A code everybody in a theatre already knows. The best kind."`,kind:"gloss",word:"DSC"},{where:"end of Chapter 3",kind:"hook",who:"STAGE",text:"Across the yard, a light comes on in Mr Dunmore's workshop. Through the window: a tall shape under a dust sheet, and Mr Dunmore's hand reaching for its corner."},{where:"Chapter 4 — The timeline",text:"The washing line runs from Monday to 8:04 on Tuesday night. This is a tight timeline: five of its cards fall inside six minutes, and the child places them by time words and by tense."},{where:"timeline: Asha's peg",text:"Dhal, 6:15 — in grey, at the far left. She is firm about it.",kind:"peg"},{where:"timeline: the line's little shake",text:"*Had* already. Before what? Read Celeste's sentence again, and find the moment it's measured from.",kind:"shake",who:"QUILL",condition:`the child pegs event 5's "had already left" after event 6`,events:["T5","T6"]},{where:"end of Chapter 4",kind:"hook",who:"ASHA",text:"After 8:02, nobody on this line is holding a lantern. Nobody. Not one card."},{where:"end of Chapter 4",kind:"hook",who:"DEV",text:"Then it's still exactly where Celeste put it."},{where:"end of Chapter 4",kind:"hook",who:"STAGE",text:"Everyone turns, slowly, to look down stage centre. The stage-door cat is sitting beside the glow tape, as it has sat all night, staring at the boards."},{where:"accusation: scene",text:"The stage itself, eleven o'clock. Half an hour to the last tram; twenty hours to curtain up. The work lights are switched off and the stage lights on, warm, as if for a show. The four suspects sit on chairs in a row along the back, Sunny with her canvas bag on her knees and her tram ticket in her fist. Petra, Ada, Mrs Achterberg and Tully sit in the stalls. The new detective sits in Row A, Seat 1. Felix stands alone, down stage centre, carefully not on the glow tape."},{where:"accusation: two halves, both required",text:"Where is the lantern? Who told a story that wasn't true?"},{where:"accusation: halvesNote",text:"This case's accusation has two halves, both required: Where is the lantern? and Who told a story that wasn't true?"},{where:"accusation: culpritNote",text:"Mr Lionel Hale told the false story: he was not in the wings during the blackout and saw nothing, because he was measuring the front of house for Silas Crane. Nobody stole the lantern. It fell through trap three and lies beneath the stage."},{where:"accusation: minimalEvidenceNotes",text:"D2 — [c:hale-wings] + [c:cel-left]: he says he was there; he had already left."},{where:"accusation: minimalEvidenceNotes",text:"D4 — [c:cue14] + [c:plan-trap3] + [c:trap-drop]: the lantern stood on a trap that drops under weight."},{where:"accusation: minimalEvidenceNotes",text:"D3 — [c:sunny-clunk] + [c:sunny-stone]: it really went down. (D1 is accepted in this slot.)"},{where:"accusation: acceptableNote",text:"D1 is accepted in the D3 slot."},{where:"accusation: acceptableFor",text:'{"D1": "D3"}'},{where:"accusation: wrong suspect sunny — points to",text:"plan-box, cue16, sunny-notorch",kind:"points"},{where:"accusation: wrong suspect dunmore — points to",text:"cel-alone, cue15, dun-struck, dun-case",kind:"points"},{where:"accusation: wrong suspect celeste — points to",text:"cel-off, cel-wobble",kind:"points"},{where:"accusation: weak evidence (lie-without-place)",text:"You have shown a man told a story. A missing thing is still missing. Where does a lantern go when nobody carries it?",who:"QUILL"},{where:"accusation: weak evidence (place-without-lie)",text:"You have found the lantern. Now: someone named a thief who was not one. Sunny deserves to hear you prove it.",who:"QUILL"},{where:"accusation: speechNote",text:`Felix's speech is placed by the script in Chapter 5, after the accusation and before the reveal ("Felix plans his speech", then "Felix's speech (in full)"). The planning step is in arc (kind "speech-plan").`},{where:"accusation: speechStage",text:"He walks to the front. The stage lights are hot. He opens his mouth, and nothing comes. Five seconds. The stage-door cat walks across the stage, sits down, and looks at him. Felix finds Row A, Seat 1, and speaks only to the new detective."},{where:"accusation: speechRubric",text:"The speech is the model for The Podium's level 3 rubric: a hook that poses the problem, a preview of three points, each point with its evidence quoted, a close that returns to the hook's image and asks for an action."}],M=[{who:"FELIX",title:"Felix's reveal speech",placement:"after the accusation, before the reveal (Chapter 5)",lines:[{slot:"hook",text:"Good evening."},{slot:"hook",text:"At two minutes past eight tonight, the lights on this stage went out for forty seconds."},{slot:"hook",text:"When they came back, a lantern that has stood here for a hundred and fifty years was gone."},{slot:"hook",text:"Nobody carried it away. Nobody hid it."},{slot:"hook",text:"And one person told us a story about it that was not true."},{slot:"hook",text:"I am going to show you three things."},{slot:"hook",text:"What Sunny heard. Where the lantern stood. And who was really in the wings."},{slot:"point",text:'In her log, Sunny wrote: "A clunk, down centre. It went down like a stone."'},{slot:"point",text:"We all read that as a review. When a joke flops, we say it went down like a stone."},{slot:"point",text:"But read it where she wrote it, straight after the clunk."},{slot:"point",text:'"It" is not the scene. "It" is the thing that clunked.'},{slot:"point",text:"Sunny wasn't being clever. She was being exact."},{slot:"point",text:"Something heavy went down."},{slot:"point",text:"Footnote — *(He stops. Laughter, kind. He smiles.)* No. No footnotes tonight."},{slot:"point",text:"This afternoon, the lantern's mark was moved to DSC. Down stage centre."},{slot:"point",text:"On Mr Dunmore's plan, down stage centre is trap three."},{slot:"point",text:"On Mr Dunmore's notice, trap three drops open under any weight, then springs shut again."},{slot:"point",text:"The new glow tape went right over his chalk cross."},{slot:"point",text:'And Sunny saw it happen. Her log says: "The glow tape winked at me. Lovely."'},{slot:"point",text:"Glow tape doesn't wink. Not unless the floor under it drops away and springs back."},{slot:"point",text:"It was in her log from the start, and we all read it as Sunny being Sunny."},{slot:"point",text:"Two good notes, by two careful people."},{slot:"point",text:"Nobody read them side by side. Until tonight."},{slot:"point",text:"Mr Hale wrote that he stood in the wings all through the blackout."},{slot:"point",text:"He wrote that he saw Sunny take the lantern."},{slot:"point",text:'But Celeste wrote: "When I came off, he had already left."'},{slot:"point",text:"*Had already.* That means before the lights went out, he was gone."},{slot:"point",text:"His own notebook puts him in the box office at two minutes past eight."},{slot:"point",text:"And Sunny's little torch is taped to her desk."},{slot:"point",text:"She never left her chair."},{slot:"close",text:"So nobody stole the Founder's Lantern."},{slot:"close",text:"The gates were locked, and nothing left the theatre, because it never left the stage."},{slot:"close",text:"It went down, not out."},{slot:"close",text:"It has been here all evening, under our feet."},{slot:"close",text:'Tonight Mrs Achterberg told us, "A lantern lit below lights those above."'},{slot:"close",text:"I thought it was a riddle about a lantern. It is."},{slot:"close",text:"It is also about everyone who works where you cannot see them."},{slot:"close",text:"The crew. The prompter. The person in the box."},{slot:"close",text:"Mr Dunmore, would you open trap three, please?"}]}],C=[{span:"note-crane",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"note-secret",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"sunny-faders",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"cel-honest",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"hale-guest",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"hale-mine",note:"markable, not needed to solve (texture, motive or a pin that leads nowhere)"},{span:"cel-dark",note:"Celeste's secret: why she always leaves just before a blackout (it clears her early exit)"}],L=[{id:"x1",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"vocab-in-context",skill:"vocab",strand:"word",objective:"w10-clues",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.3",span:"plan-key"},prompt:`The stage plan says:
"D is down, nearest the audience. U is up, at the back. C is centre."
"S is stage."
So what does DSC mean?`,options:["down stage centre","dark stage corner","door, stairs, curtain"],answer:"down stage centre",explain:"Theatre people use short letters. Put the plan's keys together: D for down, S for stage, C for centre. So DSC is the middle of the stage, right at the front."},{id:"x2",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"pronoun",skill:"pronoun",strand:"sentence",objective:null,chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.5",span:"hale-torch"},prompt:`Mr Hale writes about "the lighting operator".
Then he writes: "I could see her by her little torch."
Who is "her"?`,options:["Celeste, the actor","Petra, the stage manager","Sunny, the lighting operator"],answer:"Sunny, the lighting operator",explain:'"Her" points back to the last person named, the lighting operator. That is Sunny. A pronoun only makes sense when you look back for the noun it stands for.'},{id:"x3",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"figurative",skill:"figurative",strand:"literature",objective:"li5-simile",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.1",span:"petra-margin"},prompt:`Petra wrote: "Sunny says cue 15 "went down like a stone". Agreed! Scene needs work."
What does Petra think Sunny meant?`,options:["The lights went too bright.","The scene went badly.","The thunder was too loud."],answer:"The scene went badly.",explain:'When a show or a joke goes badly, people say it "went down like a stone". It is a simile: it uses "like" to compare. Petra read it that way, because "Scene needs work" follows.'},{id:"x4",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"spelling",skill:"vocab",strand:"word",objective:null,chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.1",span:null},prompt:`The cue sheet says: "Dress ___, Tuesday."
Which spelling is right?`,options:["rehearsal","rehersal","rehearsel"],answer:"rehearsal",explain:'Look for "hear" inside it: re-HEAR-sal. A rehearsal is when actors practise, and everyone hears the play again and again.'},{id:"x5",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"sentence-combine",skill:"punctuation",strand:"sentence",objective:"s6-join",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.2",span:null},prompt:`Mr Dunmore wrote: "Do not stand on it. Do not put anything on it."
Which one sentence keeps both rules?`,options:["Do not stand on it, but put anything on it.","Do not stand on it or put anything on it.","Stand on it and do not put anything on it."],answer:"Do not stand on it or put anything on it.",explain:'"Or" joins two things you must not do. "But" would mean you may put things on it, which is the opposite. Joining short rules makes them quicker to read, without losing one.'},{id:"x6",tier:"easier",level:2,type:"tense-sequence",skill:"sequence",strand:"reading",objective:null,chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.1",span:"cue14"},prompt:`Read the cue sheet.
Put these in the order they happened.`,options:["Blackout.","Wren sets the lantern down.","Lights up, cold.","Wren exits."],answer:["Wren sets the lantern down.","Wren exits.","Blackout.","Lights up, cold."],explain:"The cue numbers and times tell you the order: cue 14 at 8:01, then Wren exits, then cue 15 at 8:02, then cue 16 forty seconds later. Times and numbers are a reader's best clock."},{id:"x7",tier:"at",level:3,type:"figurative",skill:"figurative",strand:"literature",objective:"li5-simile",chapter:2,source:{doc:"7.S1.Q2",span:"sunny-down"},prompt:`Petra read "went down like a stone" as a review.
But Sunny says: "I meant the clunk. Something heavy, down centre."
What did Sunny mean?`,options:["Something heavy really dropped.","The scene was a flop.","The lights went out too fast.","The thunder came late."],answer:"Something heavy really dropped.",explain:"The same words can be a comparison or a plain fact. Sunny was not judging the scene. She meant it literally: something heavy fell, like a stone falls. Always read a line where it was written."},{id:"x8",tier:"at",level:3,type:"vocab-in-context",skill:"vocab",strand:"word",objective:"w10-clues",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.2",span:"trap-drop"},prompt:`Mr Dunmore's notice says:
"Put any weight on it and it drops open, then springs shut again."
What does "springs shut" mean?`,options:["opens slowly, a little at a time","breaks into pieces","closes again by itself, quickly","stays wide open"],answer:"closes again by itself, quickly",explain:'A spring jumps back into place. So "springs shut" means the trap closes by itself, fast. After it shuts, you could not tell it had ever opened.'},{id:"x9",tier:"at",level:3,type:"pronoun",skill:"pronoun",strand:"sentence",objective:null,chapter:2,source:{doc:"7.S2.Q4",span:"dun-glass"},prompt:`Asked about the lantern, Mr Dunmore says:
"No! It's a hundred and fifty years old. It belongs in a glass case, not in a play."
What does "it" stand for?`,options:["the Founder's Lantern","the stage","the glass case"],answer:"the Founder's Lantern",explain:'The question was about the lantern, so "it" points back to the lantern. A pronoun can point back to a word in the question, not only in the answer.'},{id:"x10",tier:"at",level:3,type:"summarise",skill:"detail",strand:"reading",objective:null,chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.2",span:"trap-loose"},prompt:"What is the main message of Mr Dunmore's notice?",options:["Trap three is loose, so keep off it until it is mended.","Somebody, probably the cat, keeps borrowing Mr Dunmore's good hammer from the workshop.","The new catch comes on Saturday."],answer:"Trap three is loose, so keep off it until it is mended.",explain:"A summary gives the one thing the writer most wants you to know. The hammer is a joke in the P.S. Saturday tells you when, but the warning is the point."},{id:"x11",tier:"at",level:3,type:"word-parts",skill:"vocab",strand:"word",objective:"w6-build",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.5",span:"hale-saw"},prompt:`Mr Hale writes: "I saw the lighting operator creep onstage."
Operate + or = operator.
What does the ending "-or" add?`,options:["more than one","a person who does it","it happened in the past","not"],answer:"a person who does it",explain:'"-or" and "-er" turn a doing word into a person. An operator operates the lights. An actor acts, and a visitor visits.'},{id:"x12",tier:"at",level:3,type:"tense-sequence",skill:"sequence",strand:"sentence",objective:"s2-tense",chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.6",span:"cel-left"},prompt:`Celeste wrote: "When I came off, he had already left."
Which happened first?`,options:["Celeste came off.","They happened at the same moment.","He left."],answer:"He left.",explain:'"Had left" is the past perfect. It points further back than "came off". "Already" makes it even clearer: by the time Celeste came off, the leaving was finished.'},{id:"x13",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"vocab-in-context",skill:"vocab",strand:"word",objective:"w10-clues",chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.7",span:"note-box"},prompt:`A notebook page says: "Box office. Too small. Knock through to the bar?"
What does "knock through" mean here?`,options:["take a wall down to join two rooms","knock politely on the box office door","walk through very quickly"],answer:"take a wall down to join two rooms",explain:'The clue is "Too small". Someone wants a bigger room, and "through to the bar" names the room next door. To knock through is to remove the wall between.'},{id:"x14",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"punctuation",skill:"punctuation",strand:"sentence",objective:null,chapter:1,source:{doc:"7.4",span:"sunny-thunder"},prompt:`Sunny's log says: "Thunder late AGAIN. Mr D, please!"
What do the capital letters in AGAIN tell the reader?`,options:["It is the name of a lighting cue","It starts a new sentence","She is cross, because it keeps happening"],answer:"She is cross, because it keeps happening",explain:'Capitals in the middle of a sentence are like a raised voice. Together with "please!" they show Sunny is fed up. Writers use them sparingly, or every word starts shouting.'},{id:"x15",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"pronoun",skill:"pronoun",strand:"reading",objective:null,chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.6",span:"cel-left"},prompt:`Celeste wrote: "When I went on, Mr Dunmore and the inspector were side by side in the wings."
Then: "When I came off, he had already left."
Then: "Mr Dunmore was alone at the thunder sheet."
Who is "he"?`,options:["Mr Dunmore, because his name comes first","The inspector, because the next line says Mr Dunmore was still there","Nobody can tell from what she wrote"],answer:"The inspector, because the next line says Mr Dunmore was still there",explain:'Two men were named, so "he" could mean either. The next sentence settles it: Mr Dunmore was still there, alone. So "he" is the inspector, Mr Hale.'},{id:"x16",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"tense-sequence",skill:"contradiction",strand:"reading",objective:"s2-tense",chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.5",span:"hale-stayed"},prompt:`Mr Hale wrote: "I did not leave the wings until the lights came up."
Celeste came off "just before the blackout" and wrote: "he had already left."
Why can't both be true?`,options:["He was gone before the lights even went out","Celeste was too far away to see the wings","The lights came up too early"],answer:"He was gone before the lights even went out",explain:'Celeste came off before the blackout, and "had already left" puts his leaving before that. So he left the wings before the dark began. He cannot also have stood there all through it.'},{id:"x17",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"figurative",skill:"figurative",strand:"literature",objective:"li5-simile",chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.4",span:"sunny-stone"},prompt:`Sunny's log: "A clunk, down centre." Then: "It went down like a stone."
Read in its place, what is "It"?`,options:["the whole scene","the thing that clunked down centre","the thunder sheet","the lights"],answer:"the thing that clunked down centre",explain:'Read a line where it was written. It comes straight after the clunk, so "It" is the heavy thing that made the clunk. Down centre was trap three. The simile was not a review at all: the lantern really went down.'},{id:"x18",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"inference",skill:"inference",strand:"reading",objective:null,chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.7",span:"note-secret"},prompt:`Mr Hale said: "I am not here to inspect anything tonight."
His notebook says: "Mr C. wants every room measured by Friday." and "Do NOT tell the theatre."
What was he really doing?`,options:["Measuring the theatre for Mr Crane, in secret","Watching the play as Mr Crane's guest","Looking for the lost lantern"],answer:"Measuring the theatre for Mr Crane, in secret",explain:'Measuring every room is inspecting, whatever he called it. "Gift shop goes here" and "Do NOT tell the theatre" show it was a plan the Playhouse was not meant to know about.'},{id:"x19",tier:"harder",level:4,type:"summarise",skill:"sequence",strand:"speaking",objective:null,chapter:5,source:{doc:"7.4",span:"sunny-stone"},prompt:`Felix needs a hook, three points and a close.
Put five of his cards in order. One card does not belong.`,options:["Who was really in the wings: 'he had already left'.","A lantern lit below lights those above.","Footnote: a complete history of trapdoors, 1500 to today.","The lights went out for forty seconds, and a lantern vanished.","Where the lantern stood: DSC is trap three.","What Sunny heard: 'It went down like a stone.'"],answer:["The lights went out for forty seconds, and a lantern vanished.","What Sunny heard: 'It went down like a stone.'","Where the lantern stood: DSC is trap three.","Who was really in the wings: 'he had already left'.","A lantern lit below lights those above."],explain:"A hook poses the problem. Each point carries one piece of evidence, quoted. The close comes back to an image and leaves the audience with something. The three points may go in any order. The history of trapdoors is interesting, but it is not evidence, so it goes in the bin."}],E=[{who:"STAGE",text:"The cork board goes up on an easel down stage left, well away from the glow tape. Constable Tully opens his notebook at a fresh page. It says DUNMORE, underlined twice."},{who:"TULLY",text:"I've been thinking. I don't do it quick, but I do do it. Mr Dunmore wants that lantern off the stage. He says so, a hundred times. He was in the wings, in the dark. He carries every key in this building on one ring."},{who:"TULLY",text:"He heard a bump *under the floor*, he says. Under the floor is his. And there's something lantern-shaped under a sheet in his workshop. I looked through the window."},{who:"ASHA",text:"And his thunder was late."},{who:"TULLY",text:"Three seconds late. Where was he for three seconds?"},{who:"NELL",direction:"sitting on her hands",text:"I'm not rating it. *(A pause.)* But if I did, it would be a big number."},{who:"QUILL",text:"Write it in pencil, Constable."}],q={format:1.1,id:e,number:7,title:t,label:n,level:3,world:o,setting:a,skills:s,vanishedWord:r,ledgerIndex:7,officeObject:i,arc:h,tutorial:l,card:d,cutscene:c,cast:u,docs:p,interviews:w,deductions:g,redHerrings:y,timeline:m,accusation:f,reveal:b,epilogue:k,drill:v,art:x,source:T,wrongLinks:I,arcMarks:S,revealSetting:A,aside:D,speeches:M,extraMarks:C,exercises:L,boardIntro:E};export{f as accusation,h as arc,S as arcMarks,x as art,D as aside,E as boardIntro,d as card,u as cast,c as cutscene,g as deductions,q as default,p as docs,v as drill,k as epilogue,L as exercises,C as extraMarks,H as format,e as id,w as interviews,n as label,F as ledgerIndex,W as level,N as number,i as officeObject,y as redHerrings,b as reveal,A as revealSetting,a as setting,s as skills,T as source,M as speeches,m as timeline,t as title,l as tutorial,r as vanishedWord,o as world,I as wrongLinks};
